.TH TOKDUMP 1 "September 2026" "tokdump" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tokdump \- dump tokens (like hexdump, for tokens)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tokdump
[\fB\-x\fR] [\fB\-\-strict\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.br
.B tokdump
[\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR]
.br
.B tokdump
[\fB\-v\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tokdump
dumps token IDs for files or standard input using OpenAI's
.B o200k_base
encoding (the encoding used by GPT\-4o and related models), in a classic
three\-column hexdump\-C style layout.
.PP
With no files,
.B tokdump
reads standard input and dumps tokens.
With one or more files, it dumps each file; with more than one file, it prints
a header line
.BI tokdump: " FILENAME" :
before each dump.
.PP
Unreadable files produce an error on stderr, other files continue, and
the exit status is non\-zero if any file failed.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
Each dump line has:
.TP
.B Offset
Token index as 7 lowercase hex digits.
.TP
.B Token IDs
Exactly 4 per row (last row may have fewer), each right\-aligned in width 7.
.TP
.B Text column
Starts at column 52. Token pieces are separated by
.BR | .
.PP
A token piece is a run of bytes, not necessarily a whole character: one emoji
routinely spans several tokens. Anything that is not plainly readable is
escaped rather than hidden, because the characters worth dumping are exactly
the ones that render as nothing.
.TP
.B ·
A space (U+00B7 MIDDLE DOT).
.TP
.B ↵
A newline (U+21B5).
.TP
.BR \et ", " \er
Tab, carriage return.
.TP
.BR \e\e ", " \e|
A literal backslash, a literal pipe. Escaping the pipe keeps the piece
separator unambiguous.
.TP
.B \exNN
A control byte, or a byte belonging to a character that is split across this
token boundary.
.TP
.BR \euXXXX ", " \eUXXXXXXXX
A codepoint that renders as nothing: zero\-width space, zero\-width joiner,
bidi control, BOM, soft hyphen, non\-break space, private use, unassigned.
.TP
.B ◌x
A combining mark with no base character before it in the piece, shown on a
dotted circle so it does not attach to the separator.
.PP
After all tokens, a trailing line prints the next offset alone.
Empty input prints only
.BR 0000000 .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-hex
Print token IDs in hexadecimal instead of decimal.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Treat input that is not valid UTF\-8 as an error instead of a warning.
The offending input is not dumped and the exit status is non\-zero.
.SH ENCODING OF INPUT
Input is expected to be UTF\-8.
.PP
Input that is not valid UTF\-8 is still dumped, using the same substitution an
API client would apply on the way out: each maximal invalid byte sequence
becomes a single U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER. This is the WHATWG rule, and it
matches Python's
.BR "bytes.decode(errors=\(dqreplace\(dq)" ,
so the token IDs agree with the reference
.B tiktoken
implementation rather than with a naive per\-byte substitution.
.PP
When this happens
.B tokdump
writes a warning to stderr before the dump. Use
.B \-\-strict
to reject such input instead.
.RB ( "tokdump <version>" )
and exit 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Dump tokens from stdin:
.B echo \-n "Hello world" | tokdump
.TP
Dump tokens in one file:
.B tokdump README.md
.TP
Dump tokens in multiple files:
.B tokdump a.txt b.txt
.SH ENCODING
v1 always uses
.BR o200k_base .
A future
.B \-e
/
.B \-\-encoding
flag may select other encodings.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hexdump (1),
.BR od (1),
.BR tc (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Omar Bohsali
